f In America which is a hard position  to hold  um and and and secondly it actually  misses something very important and as I  say my column and spectator this week  one of the remarkable things of the  constitutional monarchy is that our our  national the inevitable National  obsession with dynasties is focused on  one Dynasty and that one that's lasted  of course More Than A Thousand Years the  institution of the monarchy  um and and to a great extent this is a a  political interest but it's also to do  with an inevitable human biological  interest which is our interests in  watching genes play out are interest in  watching generation after generation of  a family where are the black sheep where  are the where are the bad eggs who are  the people who really are the the  inheritors of great um figures of the  past and much more you know who's got  her her side of the family more who's  got his side of the family more these  are sort of interesting conversations we  all have in our lives with about friends  and Friends children and so on but  nationally that Obsession focuses solely  on the institution of monarchy and we  don't have as a result uh political  dynasties or dynasties As Americans  would put it we don't have that we we  have a little bit of it uh on the  political left interestingly enough the  Ben and the family you might say Harriet  Harman of the pakenhams uh you might say  uh the middle a little bit of  milibandian and of course rather  remarkably a little bit of hereditary  cynicism which is a quite amazing and  unforeseeable  um uh hereditary line in politics that  one is international that power family  and that one's gone internationally  exactly who would have predicted that 30  years ago  um but you know the  um we don't have political identities  nobody's obsessed in Britain with  whether or not John Major's children are  going to come back and save us or you  know whether the players are going to  run for office again whereas in America  politics is very dominated by a few  dynasties people are obsessed with it  and you don't have you know you can go  back to the founding and the 19th  century in America you could be Adams  Dynasty in American politics that you  can you can look at the the Roosevelts  for instance uh becoming more up to date  the Kennedys uh even more up to date  look at the way in which over the last  25 years it's looked at times as if the  presidency is simply going to rotate  between the Clinton family and the Bush  family uh you know a a look at the way  in which people wonder whether Michelle  Obama is going to run for office is  Chelsea Clinton going to run at some  point are the bushes going to offer us  up a new generation of talent  um and even though even the disruptors  in American politics Donald Trump is Don  Jr going to run you know think of this  in British terms and it seems absurd you  know  um are the Johnson children being lined  up for political office I would think  not  um uh so so as I say in the piece one of  the many many things that constitutional  monarchy does that is so good is it it  actually detoxifies the the political  Arena from having hereditary politics  hereditary electoral politics and you  know as well as providing us with  constitutional order and much much more  I think this is a little noted but  important role that the monarchy  provides it does seem like such  extraordinary and strong pattern in  American politics now you've already  mentioned this human desire obsession  with seeing how genes play out but do  you think there's something else here  that means that America is so prone to  these dynasties you know what's going on  you know this is no longer a coincidence  yes that's right well there are some  explanations you could say there for  instance running for the top job in  America means you've got to be able to  bundle a lot of money you've got to have  a lot of connections nationally across  all the states and much more and I  suppo